  <!-- ========== 3. 24-48H DELTA (descriptive, verb-disciplined) ========== -->
  <div class="section" id="delta">
    <h2 class="section-title">
      What Moved (Last 24-48h)
      <span class="meta">Descriptive · banned-verb lint enforced · each paragraph cites a forecast or is marked context-only</span>
    </h2>
    <div class="delta-narrative">
      <p>The 60-day Islamabad Memorandum of Understanding expired on August 17 without a final US–Iran agreement. The Times of Israel on August 17 reported that both sides ended the MOU period "further apart than before," with Iran's demand for a permanent Hormuz transit-fee regime incompatible with the US position on freedom of navigation. ABC News on August 25 reported that messages between Tehran and Washington pass through intermediaries only, with Iran declining to characterize this as formal negotiation; Trump described the US as "semi-negotiating" and said formal talks could restart "at some point." → moved <span class="cite">F2</span>, <span class="cite">F4</span></p>

      <p>The Strait of Hormuz recorded a single commercial transit on August 16 against a pre-war baseline of approximately 73 per day. Iran published a blacklist of 46 vessels on August 24–25, threatening fines, detention or seizure; the South Florida Conservative "State of the Strait" tracker (week ending August 25) confirmed the coercive access regime has extended to financial penalties, with Iran working to transform wartime transit control into a structured fee-and-permission system. Brent crude traded at $91.36 on August 25 against pre-war levels near $70. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span></p>

      <p>The United States imposed new sanctions on Hezbollah on August 20, extending economic pressure beyond Iran's government to its affiliated networks. On August 17, Trump threatened Oman with unspecified consequences if it interfered with US efforts in the strait — a reference to Iran's ongoing bilateral transit-route talks with Muscat. The UK–France-led multinational demining coalition of 15 nations remained in a finalization phase through late July (gCaptain), and National Defense Magazine reported on July 27 that the US Navy was still determining its operational approach to mine countermeasures in the strait. → moved <span class="cite">F3</span>, <span class="cite">F1</span></p>

      <p>Israel's October 27 legislative election remains on schedule. The Knesset formally dissolved July 17, and Netanyahu's coalition is running as a unified bloc for the mandatory date. Haaretz published a poll on August 6 showing Netanyahu's Likud at 21 mandates against Eisenkot's Yashar at 23, with the opposition projected at 59–69 total seats against the coalition's 51; a Kan public broadcaster poll showed neither bloc holding a majority. Netanyahu remains PM in caretaker status. → supports <span class="cite">F7</span></p>

      <p>Iran's talks with Oman over a Hormuz transit-route arrangement continued as of August 4 (CNN), with Iran seeking temporary arrangements — lasting up to three months — to control a preapproved transit route including fees; Oman has not confirmed agreement to any terms, and the US warned against any arrangement it views as Iranian transit control. This bilateral track is the most active diplomatic channel open as of August 26. → moved <span class="cite">F2</span>, <span class="cite">F3</span></p>
    </div>
  </div>

